ds in time indicates that the  assumptions made by the models about how  the atmosphere will respond to changes  in greenhouse gas concentrations are  appropriate they're accurate the paper  also looked at older predictions  including some made in the 1970s being  much older and so much more primitive  you'd expect these predictions to be  less accurate but compare the warming  observed since a model was created in  blue with the warming predicted by that  model in red some models overestimate  the warming the red dot is higher than  the blue while others underestimate the  warming but for almost all of the models  considered their predictions fall within  the error bars of observed warming in  other words even old relatively  primitive predictions of future climate  change have thus far been largely  accurate and further each model here  represents the earth's system slightly  differently with some processes like  cloud formation or feedbacks and how  forests are responding to a change in  climate represented with different  numbers attached in the computer code  and this is why climate scientists and  indeed meteorologists use what we call  ensembles collections of statistical  models none of which are correct per se  but each of which are a good  approximation of reality combine them  together though and you get something  that is greater than the sum of its  parts not a perfect representation of  reality but a robust statistical model  of it something that we can use to make  predictions so if past predictions of  climate change have been broadly  accurate and borne out by the data which  indicates that our current predictions  are probably going to be also  why do we think of climate change  predictions as being well pants well  that has more to do with us as humans  rather than the climate models  themselves firstly any long-term  prediction needs definitionally a long  time to verify a prediction about what  the next 10 years will look like needs  10 years in order to work out if it was  correct or not by which point almost  everybody would have a forgotten the  prediction was made in the first place  and b forgotten exactly what that  prediction said you combine that with  the fact that science was within  statistically acceptable bounds  again isn't exactly headline news we  almost never hear of the long-term  verification of predictions perhaps in  the absence of verification we humans if  we even remember that a prediction was  made in the first place just tend to  assume that well it was probably wrong  add to that the fact that we as humans  are terrible at treating predictions and  indeed facts  neutrally psychology experiments have  shown that humans often only accept  something as true  if it supports what they want to believe  and something called motivated reasoning  we think this is because it's  psychologically easier to reconcile any  contradictions between the evidence in  front of you and your feelings on a  subject than it is to revise those  feelings that's something that takes a  lot of internal energy the predictions  made by climate scientists that the  world is fundamentally changing getting  warmer with more extreme weather are  scary and there are a lot of people who  simply don't want them to be true  and further there are plenty of people  who don't want those predictions to be  true because if they were  it would threaten their way of life this  brings us back to hansen's prediction  and this graph because  here's the thing  hanson didn't make one prediction  he made three  one assumed that greenhouse gas  emissions would increase exponentially  from 1988 one assumed that emissions  would increase but linearly and the last  assumed that emissions would peak around  the year 2000 and then decrease  comparing all three predictions to  observations we see that hanson's  scenario b  is pretty close and that additionally  hanson correctly predicted the pattern  of global warming with some areas  warming faster than others and the  arctic warming fastest of all in other  words he did a pretty great job yet the  hanson plot is frequently shown with  only scenario a the most unrealistic and  the furthest from observations and this  deliberate omission is done to peddle a  narrative that climate science doesn't  know what it's doing and that global  warming isn't real  and this is frankly  willful distortion of very good science  to further an agenda that agenda is to  keep things running pretty much as they  are right now enriching the people that  are currently being enriched mostly  through the burning of fossil fuels  the next time that you see this figure  being shared or one that makes a similar  kind of argument have a look at who is  sharing it why they are sharing it and  if you're feeling particularly motivated  where their money is coming from because  it's almost certain that ultimately  money is coming from fossil fuel  interest groups i've said it before and  i'll say it again go and read merchants  of doubt by nomia rescues and eric  conway it's really powerful stuff that  just might change the way that you look  at how global warming and several other  topics are covered in the news the  reason that we think climate change  predictions are pants isn't because of  the quality of the predictions but  rather which predictions we are shown if  you want to believe that climate change  isn't real you likely follow news  sources that only show you the  predictions on the extreme ends of the  spectrum either saying that minimal  warming will take place or that an  absurd level of warming will take place  and thus that scientists clearly don't  know what they're doing or of course you  can just take stuff out of context or  willfully misrepresent it that works too  ultimately though for the past four or  five decades the science has become  increasingly clear the predictions have  been borne out by the evidence  scientists predicted that the world  would warm and it has at the rate that  most models predicted it would  this may be an uncomfortable even an  inconvenient truth  but it is still the truth we are in  trouble whether you want to believe that  or not we can trust the science and we  can believe the predictions that our  models are currently making  so let's do something  together  to avoid some of the potential futures  that we see before us  do you find graphs like this quite  intimidating statistical literacy or  lack thereof is something that's been in  the news a lot recently knowing how to  read graphs like this one or being able  to see where statistics have been  misused like in this case or this case  or this case  my god has become an increasingly  valuable skill if you want to learn what  you're being shown actually means rather  than just being told then go and check  out the course statistics fundamentals  on brilliant who have kindly sponsored  this video brilliant is an educational  app and website that teaches you new  skills or allows you to freshen up old  ones through interactive courses the  idea is to get far away from rote  learning and memorization and instead  learn by doing introducing you to a  subject through interactive exercises  and getting you to do practice problems  give it a try based on your  understanding if you get it wrong that's  no problem you've just improved your  understanding by trying brilliant has  dozens of professionally written courses  on subjects in science maths and  computer science so if you'd rather  learn a new skill like python or  supplement you're learning in the  classroom when doing calculus there's  something for everyone i went on holiday  recently and actually tried out their  course on quantum computing on my phone  and it was really fun the app works  super smoothly and it was a far more  productive way to spend time on a road  trip than just playing slay the spire  over and over again to try it for  yourself head to berlin.org simon clark  li